SCENERIO:
========
let us suppose, we have 4 instances in a subnet. 2 instances belongs to one client and 2 instances belong to another client. one instance in that 2 instances is app and 
another is database respectively. now we have implemented NSG's between 2 of the client instances to communicate with each other. and also a ZPR policy to restrict the 
communication between the client instances. client A instances can communicate within them seamlessly. so, don't consider connections within the same client. also ignore
the zpr within the same client. now ultimately what will happen ? 


##############################################################################################################################################################################

USECASE 1: NSG ALLOW AND ZPR DENY.
==================================
Think of the controls as conditions that all need to permit the traffic:
------------------------------------------------------------------------

              App-A
                │
                ▼
        ┌─────────────────┐
        │ Routing works?  │
        └────────┬────────┘
                 │
                 ▼
        ┌─────────────────┐
        │ NSG/SL allows?  │
        └────────┬────────┘
                 │
                 ▼
        ┌─────────────────┐
        │ ZPR allows?     │
        └────────┬────────┘
                 │
                 ▼
               DB-B

If ZPR denies the A→B communication:
------------------------------------
App-A ──X──► DB-B
       ZPR DENY

the connection does not succeed, even if the NSG permits it.


NOTE: ZPR doesn't override an NSG allow, and an NSG allow doesn't override a ZPR restriction. The traffic needs to satisfy the applicable network and ZPR controls.

USECASE 2: NSG DENY AND ZPR ALLOW.
=================================
App-A ─────────► DB-B

NSG:
Client A → Client B : ❌ DENY

ZPR:
Client A → Client B : ✅ ALLOW


RESULT:
=======
NSG   → DENY
ZPR   → ALLOW
──────────────
Final → ❌ DENY
##############################################################################################################################################################################
OUTCOME: 
=======

| NSG     | ZPR     | Result     |
| ------- | ------- | ---------- |
| ✅ Allow | ✅ Allow | ✅ Allowed* |
| ✅ Allow | ❌ Deny  | ❌ Denied   |
| ❌ Deny  | ✅ Allow | ❌ Denied   |
| ❌ Deny  | ❌ Deny  | ❌ Denied   |

CONCLUSION: Both must allow the communication. One denial is enough to block it.

--> SIMPLY A "LOGICAL AND".
--> A logical AND is a mathematical and programming operation that returns true only when all of its input conditions are true.

